[[es:Mito japonés de la creación de los dioses]]
[[ja:神産み]]

[[File:Izanagi Izanami genealogía.PNG|thumb|300px|Genealogy of Izanagi and Izanami according to the [[Kojiki]]]]
In [[Japanese mythology]], the story of the {{nihongo|birth of the gods|神産み|'''Kamiumi'''}} occurs after the creation of Japan (''[[Kuniumi]]'') and refers to the birth of the ''[[kami]]'', descendants of [[Izanagi]] and [[Izanami]].

==Story==
According to the [[Kojiki]], various gods were born from the relationship between Izanagi and Izanami until the fire deity, Kagutsuchi, at birth burned Izanami's genitals and wounded her fatally. Izanagi, witnessing the death of his beloved wife, in rage takes the ten-grasp<ref group="nb">A "grasp" is the breadth of four fingers when the hand is clenched.</ref> sabre and crushes his child, Kagusuchi. A number of gods were born from the blood and remains of Kagutsuchi. Subsequently, Izanagi went to the land of [[Yomi]] (the world of the dead) to find Izanami, however when he found her, she had become a rotting corpse and from her parts other gods had arisen, causing the flight of Izanagi to the world of the living. Then Izanagi performs the [[misogi]] [[ritual purification]] through which more gods are born.  The last of these are the three most important gods of [[Shinto]]: [[Amaterasu]], goddess of the sun; [[Tsukuyomi]], deity of the moon; and [[Susanoo]], god of the sea.

===Birth of the gods===
[[File:Izanami genealogía.PNG|thumb|300px|Gods born during the agony and death of Izanami.]]
After having created the Eight Large Islands (''[[Ōyashima]]'') and other islands during the [[Kuniumi|creation of Japan]], Izanagi and Izanami decided to give birth to other gods, among them the {{nihongo|''Katakurokushin''|家宅六神}}, deities of the wind, trees and meadows, all born spontaneously:

